Fundamental Geometric Constants, Variables, and Parameters
The RHUFT framework utilizes fundamental geometric constants (\[Phi],\[Pi],e) \
alongside CODATA-defined physical constants to derive a set of dimensionless \
geometric ratios.
A. Core Geometric Constants (Dimensionless)
RHUFT updated 2 1
RHUFT updated 2 2
Chapter 1: The Dimensional Foundation and Mass Emergence
1.1 The Recursive Coherence Field (\[CapitalOmega])
The fundamental shift in the Recursive Harmonic Unified Field Theory (RHUFT) \
is the dimensional definition of the primary field quantity, \
\[CapitalPsi]unified. To resolve dimensional inconsistencies in the core \
field equations, this quantity is redefined not as a traditional wave \
amplitude or density, but as the
Instantaneous Coherence Frequency
\[CapitalOmega](r,t;L).
Dimensional Correction (\[CapitalOmega]\[Congruent]T\[Minus]1)
The field \[CapitalOmega] is defined as the rate at which the field \
recursively samples and influences its future self. This allows the argument \
of the field's self-referential exponential kernel to be dimensionless, as \
originally intended (referencing the dimensional analysis in the provided \
context):
The field \[CapitalOmega] is therefore a measure of
information processing rate
within the spacetime manifold.
1.2 The General RHUFT Mass Equation
Mass (M) in RHUFT is not an intrinsic particle property but an emergent \
phenomenon resulting from the
integrated coherence density
of the field, defined as the difference between the particle's localized \
recursive field (\[CapitalOmega]rec) and the surrounding universal base field \
(\[CapitalOmega]base).
